  "strategy": "We'll use a combination of temperature annealed mirror descent and coordinate descent to optimize the sequence. First, we'll initialize with a sequence from the parent if available. We'll then run two optimization phases: mirror descent and coordinate descent. After that, we'll use peak shaving to further refine the sequence. We'll also explore alternative sequence lengths and apply a temperature schedule on the flips during mirror descent to avoid brute-force search while maintaining performance.\n\nSteps:\n1. Initialize best_sequence with parent if available, otherwise with random uniform distribution.\n\n2. Mirror Descent phase:\n   - Compute initial sequence score\n   - For each iteration, do temperature annealing by decreasing eta_start exponentially\n   - Update sequence using log space softmax weights\n   - Compute gradient using FFT\n   - Use multiplicative update to minimize the maximum convolution value\n\n3. Coordinate Descent phase:\n   - Find coordinates that contribute most to the convolution peak\n   - Iteratively update these coordinates with optimized values\n\n4. Peak Shaving step:\n   - Reduce coefficients contributing to the highest convolution values\n\n5. Length exploration:\n   - Consider alternative sequence lengths (half and double) if we have time\n\n6. Temperature tuning:\n   - End on a cooling schedule for temperature during flips\n\nImplementation details:\n\nAll functions will be independent to ensure execution in the specified time limits.\n\nKey considerations:\n- The sequence must be non-negative and sum must be at least 0.01\n- We use FFT for efficient convolution computation\n- Temperature annealing helps escape local minima\n- Multiplicative updates preserve non-negativity\n- Coordinate descent focuses on important coordinates\n\nWe'll write the following helper functions and use them in the search procedure.\n\n1. Fast convolution using FFT for efficiency\n2. Score computation using the evaluator\n3. Softmax weight calculation\n4. Coordinate descent for local optimization\n5. Peak shaving to target high contribution values\n\nThe fallback sequence will be returned if the search doesn't find anything better than the initial.\n\nLet's now write the detailed specification in steps.",
